# =============================================================================
# Kaiyo — Environment Variables
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to `.env.local` for local development:
#     cp .env.example .env.local
# In production set these in the hosting dashboard
# (Vercel: Project > Settings > Environment Variables).
#
# NEVER commit a filled-in copy of this file. `.env*` is already git-ignored
# except for `.env.example`.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site URL (SEO metadata, sitemap, and the CSRF origin check on admin writes)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Admin mutations are rejected unless the request's Origin
# host matches this value, so an unset/incorrect value here makes every admin save
# fail with a confusing 403. The app refuses to boot in production without it.
#
# Local development: http://localhost:3000
# Production:        https://your-domain.example
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database  (REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MongoDB connection string.
#
# Production (MongoDB Atlas - SRV):
#   mongodb+srv://<db_username>:<db_password>@kaiyo.vnvfmsm.mongodb.net/<database_name>?retryWrites=true&w=majority
#
# Local development:
#   mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/kaiyo
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. There is no fallback: if this is missing the app refuses
# to start rather than silently connecting to a local database. In development only,
# an unset value falls back to mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/kaiyo.
#
# IMPORTANT
#  * Never commit a filled-in value. Put the real credentials in `.env.local`
#    (git-ignored) or in the hosting provider's environment settings.
#  * `<db_password>` must be URL-encoded: @ / : ? # & and % must be escaped
#    (e.g. `p@ss` -> `p%40ss`), otherwise the driver mis-parses the URI.
#  * The URI is never logged or echoed. `backend/lib/env.ts` validates its shape
#    and refuses to include the value in any error message.
#
# Atlas setup (see docs/BACKEND.md):
#  1. Database > Access Control > Database Users -> add a user with
#     "Read and write to any database" (NOT Atlas Admin, which is unnecessary).
#  2. Network Access -> allow the deployment's egress:
#       - Vercel: add the project's static outbound IPs, or "Allow access from
#         anywhere" as a temporary diagnostic only.
#       - Local development: allow your own public IP (0.0.0.0/0 only temporarily).
#  3. Copy the SRV string from the Atlas "Connect" dialog into `.env.local`.
MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://<db_username>:<db_password>@kaiyo.vnvfmsm.mongodb.net/<database_name>?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# Local-only alternative (leave commented out when using Atlas):
# MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/kaiyo

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only secrets  (REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate each with:   openssl rand -base64 48
# or:                   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# These must be at least 32 characters and must NEVER be prefixed with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that prefix ships the value to the browser.
#
# The app FAILS TO BOOT in production if either is missing, rather than falling
# back to a guessable default.
#
# Rotating SESSION_SECRET/CSRF_SECRET invalidates nothing on its own (sessions
# live in the database), but rotate them if either is ever exposed.

# Binds the CSRF token to the session token.
CSRF_SECRET=replace-with-a-cryptographically-random-string-at-least-32-chars

# Session-related HMAC secret. Rotating it signs out nobody by itself, but it
# should be unique per environment.
SESSION_SECRET=replace-with-a-cryptographically-random-string-at-least-32-chars

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trusted reverse proxy  (optional; NOT needed on Vercel)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rate limiter derives a client IP from `x-forwarded-for` / `x-real-ip`.
# Those headers are client-controlled unless something overwrites them, so they
# are IGNORED unless this app is known to sit behind such a thing.
#
# VERCEL  ->  nothing to do. Vercel overwrites `x-forwarded-for` from the TCP
#            connection (to prevent IP spoofing) and sets `VERCEL=1` itself, so
#            the app detects this at runtime and trusts the header automatically.
#
# SELF-HOSTED behind your own proxy -> set to 1, but ONLY if the proxy REPLACES
#            the header. With nginx use:
#                proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
#            NOT the `$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for` variant, which appends to
#            whatever the caller sent and therefore leaves the leftmost entry
#            attacker-controlled.
#
# BARE NODE / no proxy -> leave unset. The app then cannot determine a client
#            address and falls back to one shared bucket, which is a real (deny)
#            limit rather than a bypassable one.
#
# CAVEAT: if another CDN sits in front of Vercel, Vercel records the *CDN's* IP,
# so every visitor shares one bucket. Real client IPs behind a proxy need
# Vercel's Verified Proxy (Enterprise) - that is a platform setting.
# TRUSTED_PROXY=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Initial administrator  (used only by scripts/create-admin.js)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are NOT read by the running application. Admin sign-in is PIN-only: the
# PIN is verified with bcrypt against the hash in the `admins` collection, and no
# credential is ever taken from the environment at runtime.
#
# `ADMIN_EMAIL` is an identity record for the account, NOT a sign-in identifier.
#
# Run once from a trusted machine:
#   ADMIN_EMAIL="you@example.com" ADMIN_PIN="123456" \
#     node backend/scripts/create-admin.js
#
# Leave ADMIN_PIN unset to be prompted interactively instead (input hidden).
# The PIN must be 4 to 12 digits. It is hashed with bcrypt (cost 12) and the
# plaintext is never stored or printed.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# ADMIN_PIN=123456
# ADMIN_NAME=Administrator

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Media storage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `local` writes to ./.uploads and is DEVELOPMENT ONLY — a serverless filesystem
# is not persistent, so the app returns 503 rather than silently losing uploads.
#
# Production options:
#   vercel-blob : requires BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN   <- RECOMMENDED
#   s3          : requires S3_UPLOAD_BASE / S3_PUBLIC_BASE (+ S3_DELETE_BASE)
MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER=local

# --- Vercel Blob ---
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=vercel_blob_rw_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- S3-compatible write endpoint ---
# NOT fully supported: AWS SigV4 signing is not implemented, so this only works
# against an endpoint that accepts an anonymous or static-token PUT (e.g. a
# self-hosted MinIO with a public-write bucket). Prefer vercel-blob.
# S3_UPLOAD_BASE=https://uploads.example.com/kaiyo
# S3_PUBLIC_BASE=https://cdn.example.com/kaiyo
# S3_DELETE_BASE=https://uploads.example.com/kaiyo
# S3_AUTH_TOKEN=optional-bearer-token

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test harness — read ONLY by backend/scripts/test-backend.js (`npm test`)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave MONGODB_URI_TEST unset to have the suite spin up an ephemeral
# `mongodb-memory-server` instead of touching any real database.

# MONGODB_URI_TEST=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/kaiyo_test

# Port for the production build the suite boots. Default 3111.
# TEST_PORT=3111

# Set to 1 to stream the Next.js server's build/start output while testing.
# TEST_VERBOSE=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tooling (not read by the application)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN is written here by the Vercel CLI for authenticated
# deployments. It is NOT application configuration and no app code reads it.

# =============================================================================
# Notes
# =============================================================================
# * There is no default admin account and no default password. Until
#   `backend/scripts/create-admin.js` has created one, POST /api/admin/login
#   always fails with a generic "Invalid credentials" and every admin route
#   returns 401.
# * The legacy ADMIN_USERNAME / ADMIN_PASSWORD / ADMIN_AUTH_SECRET variables are
#   no longer read by the application and should be removed from your deployment.
# * Only NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL is exposed to the browser. Everything else in this
#   file is read server-side only.
# * After the first production deploy, run `npm run db:indexes -- --check` to
#   confirm the indexes exist (production does not create them automatically).
# =============================================================================